message: If you use this work, please cite it as below.
title: 'Integration in the Capillary Bed: Paired-Input Predictions for Electrical Signaling to Arterioles'
authors:
- name: PIATRA Institute
date-released: '2026-11-01'
url: https://piatra.institute/papers/integration-in-the-capillary-bed.pdf
repository-code: https://github.com/piatra-institute/papers-integration-in-the-capillary-bed
abstract: Can paired capillary inputs distinguish distributed electrical interaction from independent sensing and a shared vascular nonlinearity? We implement a literature-constrained membrane network, screen single-exposure magnitude and recovery, fit rivals to isolated-input responses, and compare 128 withheld paired protocols. The central measurement contrasts sibling and distant input pairs with matched single-site waveforms. Uniqueness of a deterministic controller driven by their identical pooled inputs forces its geometry contrast to zero, irrespective of shared output saturation. The nominal distributed model predicts a 0.833 mV contrast under simultaneous stimulation; a conditional planning calculation gives 46 independent observations per condition. Concentration sweeps reveal a nearby sign reversal, while a bounded pooled filter fits poorly and localization ablations retain geometry effects. Only 29 of 8192 sampled vectors pass the initial screen, all above the reported mean-plus-or-minus-SD Kir conductance band. Slow Kir suppression improves recovery near the nominal point but yields no full-screen passes on the tested conductance-band grid. The contribution is an invariant null and a quantified synthetic discrimination design. Its physiological application requires measured source waveforms, preparation-specific timing and a reversible membrane regime compatible with the observed conductances.
